ZIF-67/PAN-800 bifunctional electrocatalyst derived from electrospun fibers for efficient oxygen reduction and oxygen evolution
## reaction
Keywords: Electrospinning; ZIF-67/PAN-800 fiber; Oxygen reduction reaction and oxygen evolution reaction; Bifunctional electrocatalyst.
## 1. Introduction
The oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER) are the two key reactions for many electrochemical energy storage and conversion technologies, such as fuel cells, metal-air batteries and water splitting1,2. The noble metal (such as Pt, Ir, and Ru)-based catalysts have been considered as the most promising electrocatalysts for ORR and/or OER3,4,5, but they have difficulties in meeting the requirements of practical applications due to their scarcity, high cost, and poor durability6. Therefore, it is important to develop highly efficient ORR/OER electrocatalysts with low cost to promote the practical applications of the energy storage and conversion technologies. In recent years, a large amount of non-noble metal catalysts for ORR/OER have been developed, including metal oxides, nanocarbons, N-doped graphene, non-N-doped metal-free carbons, edge-doped/functionalized graphene and their composites7,8,9. Among them, metal coordinating nitrogen-doped carbon catalysts, which not only can effectively compensable for the intrinsic deficiency in conductivity of  metal component, but also can induce a synergistic effect between metal and nitrogen-doped carbon, have been identified as the most promising substitution of the noble metal catalysts10. However, the metal-based catalysts with large particles result in their inadequate catalytic activity. Meanwhile, the agglomeration of metal particles lead to reduced the stability of the catalysts. Recent studies have shown that the ORR/OER catalysts with well-dispersed nanoparticles can be obtained through heat treating metal-organic framework (MOF) precursors11,12,13. MOFs, an emerging class of crystalline materials with ultrahigh specific surface area and high porosity14, are used as catalytic materials prepared through a certain processing with smaller and homogeneous dispersed metal particles. For example, Xia et al. prepared nitrogen-doped carbon nano polyhedrons decorated with cobalt nanoparticles via pyrolysis of $300~\mathrm{nm}$ ZIF-67 precursor, which exhibited the excellent ORR activity with an onset potential of 0.86 V and a half-wave potential of 0.71 V. They also found that as the particle size decreased, the catalytic activity increased15. Bo You et al. prepared the Co-P/NC catalysts by direct pyrolysis of ZIF-67 followed by phosphidation, and $\mathrm{CoP_{x}}$ nanoparticles with about $33~\mathrm{nm}$ size guaranteed the efficient exposure of catalytic active sites, which exhibited remarkable catalytic performance for both HER and OER in 1.0 M KOH 16.
Arshad Aijaz et al reported highly active ORR/OER bifunctional electrocatalysts obtained by the pyrolysis of ZIF-67 in a $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ atmosphere and subsequent controlled oxidative calcination. The catalysts could afford $0.85\mathrm{~V~}$ reversible overvoltage in 0.1M KOH, as one of the best non-noble metal electrocatalysts for reversible oxygen electrodes17. From these preliminary researches, the catalysts with small and homogeneous dispersed particles, large specific surface area, and high nitrogen content can be obtained by heat treatment of MOFs. However, the electrical conductivity of the catalysts is still relatively poor due to the presence of metal oxide and limited carbon source from organic ligands, which is unfavorable for the electrocatalysis18. In addition, the catalysts structure of MOFs could be collapsed after a heat treatment, thus these particles are easy to be aggregated during the reaction process, resulting in decreased catalytic activity and stability. Therefore, effective strategy is required to overcome these above problems.
Electrospinning is a novel processing technique developed in recent years19-20. Electrospinning followed by carbonization is a simple and efficient method to prepare carbon nanofiber composites with a high surface-area-to-volume ratio, superior mechanical property and flexibility21. Such carbon nanofibers could be used as a support carrier or nitrogen-doped carbon for ORR/OER catalysts. For example, Ren et al. prepared porous core-shell $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}$ embedded N-doped carbon nanofibers by electrospinning followed by carbonization as an effective ORR electrocatalyst22. Liu et al. also prepared nanoporous carbon fiber membranes by electrospinning followed by carbonization as bifunctional catalytic electrodes for flexible Zn-Air batteries23.
Herein, we designed a novel in-situ method to synthesis nanocomposite fibers for ORR/OER catalysis (shown in Figure 1): polyacrylonitrile (PAN) nanofibers were prepared by electrospinning firstly; ZIF-67 nanoparticles were then in-situ grown uniformly on the surface of the PAN nanofibers, named as ZIF-67/PAN fibers; ZIF-67/PAN-800 nanocomposite fiber was obtained by the carbonization of ZIF-67/PAN fibers at $800~^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ . ZIF-67/PAN-800 fiber had a uniform distribution of nanoparticles embedded on the surface of fibers. The inner carbon fibers elevate conductivity, hinder the metal particles aggregation, which is conductive to stability. And the Co-Nx structure was recognized as active site, which is beneficial for catalytic property.  Therefore, the synergism of metal particles and carbon fiber can improve the catalytic performance. Based on the electrochemical tests,
ZIF-67/PAN-800 exhibited superior activity and robust stability toward both ORR and OER in alkaline electrolyte.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1 Materials
All the chemicals were analytical grade and used without further purification, typically, polyacrylonitrile (PAN, $\mathrm{M}_{\mathrm{w}}{=}90\ 000$ , Beijing Yili Fine Chemical), cobalt nitrate hexahydrate $({\bf C o}({\bf N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ $99\%$ purity, Sigma), 2-methylimidazole (2MI, $99\%$ , Aladdin), methanol $(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ , $99.5\%$ , Tianjin Chemicals), N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.5\%$ , Tianjin Chemicals).
## 2.2 Sample preparations
## 2.3 Structure characterizations
The crystalline structures of the samples were determined by X-ray diffraction (XRD; Rigaku D/MAX-YA) using Cu Kα radiation $\langle\lambda=0.154~\mathrm{nm})$ in the scan range of $2\uptheta=$ $3{-}80^{\circ}$ ). The morphology of the samples was observed on scanning electron microscopy (SEM; JSM-6360LA, Japan) at an acceleration voltage of $15{\mathrm{~kV}},$ and transmission electron microscopy (TEM; JEM-2100, Japan) operated at $200\ \mathrm{kV}$ accelerating voltage. The elemental analysis of the samples was carried out using an X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS; Thermo Electron Corporation, Escalab 250, Germany) and XPS peak software was to correct the element binding energy.
## 2.4 Electrochemical measurements
The electrochemical measurements of these catalysts were performed in a three-electrode system which connected to a CHI 604E (Chenhua Instrucment, China) electrochemical workstation. The electrolyte used the 0.1 M KOH aqueous solution. A carbon rod and KCl-saturated Ag/AgCl electrode acted as the counter and reference electrodes, respectively, and a glassy carbon electrode loaded the target catalyst was the working electrode.
To prepare the working electrode, a homogeneous ink of the catalyst was prepared first. $2.5~\mathrm{{\mg}}$ of the catalyst sample and $2.5~\mathrm{{\mg}}$ of carbon black were ultrasonically dispersed in the mixed liquor of 1 mL ethanol and $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ Nafion (5 wt%， Dupont) to obtain a homogeneous ink. Then ${\sim}20\upmu\mathrm{L}$ ink was coated onto a glassy carbon electrode with a geometric area of $0.196~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , and the electrode loading is about $0.24~\mathrm{mg\cdotcm^{-2}}$ . The working electrode was finally obtained after drying at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ . The mass and process of preparing ink of catalyst of $20\mathrm{\wt}\%$ Pt/C was same as the samples.
All electrochemical measurements were carried out at room temperature and all the potentials discussed below are relative to the KCl-saturated $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}$ electrode (vs. Ag/AgCl).
For the ORR measurements, cyclic voltammetrys (CVs) were obtained in $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ -saturated 0.1 M KOH electrolyte in the potential range of $-1.0\sim0.2\:\mathrm{V}$ with the sweep rate of $50\ \mathrm{mV\cdots^{-1}}$ until to obtain a stable CV profile. Then the background current was obtained using the CV which carried out in ${\bf N}_{2}$ -saturated 0.1 M KOH at the same potential range but with a sweep rate of $5\ \mathrm{mV\cdots^{-1}}$ . And then the ORR catalytic activity was tested using linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) in $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ -saturated 0.1 M KOH electrolyte. The LSV profiles were obtained under the 400, 625, 900, 1225, 1600, 2025 and $2500~\mathrm{rpm}$ at the sweep rate of ${5\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}}^{-1}$ and the potential range of - $\cdot1.0\mathrm{~\sim~}0.2\mathrm{~V}.$ . The resulting current subtracted the background current in the ${\bf N}_{2}$ -saturated electrolyte is considered to be the ORR activity current.
As for OER measurements, the CVs were carried out in ${\bf N}_{2}$ -saturated 0.1 M KOH electrolyte in the potential range of $0.2\sim1.0~\mathrm{V}$ at a sweep rate of $50~\mathrm{mV\cdots^{-1}}$ until to obtain a stable CV profile. Then the OER LSV were carried out in ${\bf N}_{2}$ -saturated 0.1 M KOH electrolyte at the sweep rate of ${5\ \mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}}$ under the 900,1225, 1600, 2025 and 2500 rpm and the potential range was also from 0.2 to $1.0\mathrm{V}.$ In the part of results and discussion, all the potentials had been converted to reversible hydrogen electrodes (RHE) unless otherwise stated.
The Koutecky-Levich (K-L) equation is always used to investigate the ORR pathway on the catalysts and the K-L equation is as follows:
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\frac{1}{\mathrm{j}}=\frac{1}{\mathrm{j_{d}}}+\frac{1}{\mathrm{j_{k}}}}\end{array}
$$
Where j is the current density of experimental measurement $\left(\mathrm{mA\cdotcm}^{-2}\right)$ , $\mathbf{j}_{\mathbf{k}}$ and jd are the kinetic current density $\left(\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\right)$ ) and the diffusion-limiting current density (mA·cm ), respectively; n is the electron transfer number per $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ molecule, $\mathbf{k}$ is the rate constant of ORR, F is the Faraday constant $(96485~\mathrm{~C{\cdot}m o l^{-1}})$ , $\mathbf{Co}_{2}$ is the concentration of oxygen in 0.1 M KOH electrolyte $(1.21{\times}10^{-6}\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ , $\mathrm{~D~}$ is the diffusion coefficient of oxygen in 0.1 M KOH electrolyte $(1.9{\times}10^{-5}\mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ and ʋ is the kinematic viscosity of the electrolyte $(0.01\mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1 Structure characterizations
The XRD patterns of the PAN, PAN-800, ZIF-67/PAN and ZIF-67/PAN-800 samples are shown in Figure. 2. It can be observed that the PAN exhibits a broad diffraction peak around $17^{\mathrm{{\circ}}}$ , corresponding to the crystallization of PAN. After pyrolysis, this peak disappeared in PAN-800. The XRD patterns of ZIF-67/PAN fiber matched the patterns of ZIF- $67^{24}$ , indicating that the ZIF-67 nanoparticles were successfully synthesized on the PAN fiber surface. For ZIF-67/PAN-800, diffraction peaks appeared at (2θ) 44 ° and 51 °, which corresponded to the (111) and (200) crystal planes of face centered cubic (fcc) Co, respectively. In addition, the diffraction peak belonging to graphitic carbon can also be observed in ZIF-67/PAN-800.
ZIF-67/PAN-800.
SEM images of PAN fiber, PAN-800, ZIF-67/PAN and ZIF-67/PAN-800 are shown in Figure 3. The PAN fiber showed smooth surface with an average diameter of $853\ \mathrm{nm}$ , ranging from 600 to $900\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 3a), and no obvious morphology changes were observed for PAN-800 fiber, but the average diameter of PAN-800 decreased to $330~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ (Figure 3b). For ZIF-67/PAN (Figure 3c), the ZIF-67 nanoparticles were grown on PAN fiber surface. The ZIF-67 nanoparticles exhibited rhombic dodecahedron shapes with particle size around 500 nm, which was in agreement well with previous observations25. After the heating treatment at $800~^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ , the ZIF-67/PAN-800 sample showed a rough surface with an average diameter of 440 nm. Furthermore, a meaningful change was the appearance of some small pores on the ZIF-67/PAN-800 surface (Figure 3d), which may be caused by the generated gases associated with the collapse of skeleton of ZIF-67.
The representative HRTEM images of the ZIF-67/PAN-800 are given in Figure 4. It is clear that nanoparticles are uniformly distributed on fiber surface. Moreover, these nanoparticles are well dispersed with a smaller particle size mainly around 15 nm in diameter. The HRTEM (Figure 4c) of a single nanoparticle shows that the well-resolved (111) lattice fringe of $0.20\mathrm{nm}$ resulted from the free metal Co, and the results are in well agreement with the above XRD patterns. The Co nanoparticles are closely surrounded by carbon, and the carbon interlayer spacing is $0.334~\mathrm{nm}$ which corresponds to the (002) plane of graphitic carbon. Based on the results above, it suggests that the ZIF-67/PAN-800 has a core-shell $\operatorname{Co}\mathcal{Q}\operatorname{C}$ structure, where Co is the core surrounded by graphitized carbon tightly.
The specific surface areas and pore size distributions were investigated by ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption measurements. As shown in Figure 5, the isotherms of ZIF-67/PAN-800 belong to a mixture isotherm type I and type-IV, which show the coexistence of micropore and mesopore. The pore size distributions obtained from the adsorption isotherm further confirm its hierarchical micro/mesoporous structure, but most of them is microspore (Figure 5b). The specific surface area of the ZIF-67/PAN -800 is $495~\mathrm{m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ , which is much larger than that of the ZIF-67-800 $(56~\mathrm{m^{2}{\cdot g^{-1}}})$ and PAN-800 $(7~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ .
According to the XPS results, the relative compositions of C, O, N, and Co of ZIF-67/PAN-800, ZIF-67-800 and PAN-800 are listed in Table 1. As it can be seen, the content of Co of ZIF-67/PAN-800 $(0.6~\%)$ is lower than that of ZIF-67-800 $(2.35~\%)$ . The content of $\mathrm{Co}$ tested by EDS listed in table S1 was higher than that tested by XPS, which could due to the fact that $\mathrm{Co}$ is embedded in carbon layer. The content of Co tested by ICP was $0.30\%$ , lower than EDS. This may be due to that the carbon-coated metal is relatively stable and it is difficult to completely dissolve the metal from the carbon fiber with aqua regia. The high resolution X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) studies of N1s of the ZIF-67/PAN-800, ZIF-67-800 and PAN-800 are shown in Figure 6. The N 1s spectrum can be divided into three types of N species, which are attributed to pyridinic $(398.3\mathrm{eV})$ , pyrrolic $(399.3\ \mathrm{eV})$ ), graphitic (401.1 eV) nitrogen. The bonding energy of graphitized nitrogen in ZIF-67/PAN has a slight shift compared with that of ZIF-67-800 and PAN-800. The high resolution Co 2p spectrum could be divided into eight species clearly (Figure 7). For ZIF-67-800, the peak at 778.5 and $794.3{\mathrm{~eV}}$ are assigned to metal ${\mathrm{Co}}^{0}$ . The peaks at 780.6, 782.4 and 786.8 eV represent ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and satellite, respectively. For ZIF-67/PAN-800, the bonding energy of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{0}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ have a negative shift compared with those of ZIF-67-800. The migration of the graphitized nitrogen and Co 2p binding energies confirms the existence of interactions between ZIF-67 and PAN during the pyrolysis.
To further analyze the content of metal $\mathrm{Co}$ , the thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA) of sample ZIF-67/PAN-800 was tested under air atmosphere. In Fig. S1, the weight of ZIF-67/PAN-800 was increased under $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which was due to the oxidation of the metal elements. Owing to oxidation of carbon, the weight of sample was reduced between 300 and $520~^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ . The weight of sample was almost constant above $520~\mathrm{^{o}C}.$ and the remaining production was corresponded to $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ $(48.2\%)$ ). According to the calculation, the content of $\mathrm{Co}$ in the sample ZIF-67/PAN-800 was $35.4\%$ .
The OER activities of as-electrocatalysts were measured in ${\bf N}_{2}$ -saturated 0.1 M KOH $\mathrm{(pH=1}3$ ) electrolyte via a RDE technique. The RDE voltammograms obtained at different rotating speeds from 400 to $2500~\mathrm{rpm}$ are shown in Fig. S2. The linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves of OER at 1600 rpm with a scan rate of 5 mV s -1 are shown on the Figure. 8, and three important parameters are summarized in Table 2.
ZIF-67-800 and PAN-800 have a lower OER catalytic activity than that of $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ . The OER current of ZIF-67-800 doesn’t raise until $1.65\mathrm{~V~}$ and the current density is only $9.1~\mathrm{\mV/cm}^{2}$ at 1.76 V. For ZIF-67/PAN-800, its catalytic activity was obviously enhanced than that of PAN-800 and ZIF-67-800. Obvious OER current of ZIF-67/PAN-800 can be observed at $1.58{\mathrm{~V~}}$ and the current density highly reached $45.1\ \mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ at 1.76 V. ZIF-67/PAN-800 has a potential of 1.64 V (Figure 8a and Table 2) at current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ (i.e., $\mathrm{E_{j=}}10$ , an important parameter for assessing OER activities26-28), which shows a more remarkable OER catalytic activity than that of ZIF-67-800 and PAN-800. In addition, ZIF-67/PAN-800 has a lower Tafel slope of $94.3~\mathrm{mV}$ dec-1 than those of PAN-800 (427.7 mV dec-1), ZIF-67 ( ${\bf95.9m V}$ dec-1) and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ $(221.3.8~\mathrm{mV}$ dec-1) (Figure 8b and Table 2), indicating a more favorable reaction kinetics during the catalytic process.
