Under a sweep rate of 5 mV s -1 in $0.1\textbf{M}$ $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ -saturated KOH electrolyte, the RDE voltammograms obtained at different rotating speeds from 400 to 2500 rpm are shown in Fig. S3. The Kouteckye-Levich (K-L) plots for the ZIF-67/PAN-800, ZIF-67-800 and PAN-800 electrocatalysts at various potentials (- 0.2 to $-0.9\mathrm{~V~}$ ) were obtained from the RDE data and Eq. (1)-(3).
The K-L plots for the electrocatalysts reveal good linear relationship between jdand $\mathbf{\omega}_{\infty}^{-1/2}$ . The approximately parallel feature with each other under the same potentials suggests that first-order reaction kinetic was occurred at the oxygen concentration in the KOH electrolyte29-30. The n is another significant kinetic metric to assess the ORR kinetic efficiency of electrocatalysts, which can be estimated from the LSV curves according to the K-L equation (Fig. S4). The n calculated from the slope of the K-L plots of ZIF-67/PAN-800 is about 3.4\~5 (Fig. S5), suggesting a four-electron pathway of ZIF-67/PAN-800 for ORR31-33. While for PAN-800 and ZIF-67-800, the electron transfer number n is only about 3.2\~3.8 and 2.8\~3.4, respectively, implying their incomplete ORR processes 30. The n and the production of hydrogen peroxide of ZIF-67/PAN-800 also can be calculated by rotating ring-disk electrode (RRDE) experiment (Fig. S7). The n tested by RRDE is consistent with that calculated by the K-L plots. The $\mathrm{HO}_{2}^{-}$ yield is below $40\%$ at $0.2\mathrm{-}0.8\mathrm{V}.$
The electrochemical double layer capacitance $\mathrm{(C_{dl})}$ was derived from CV curves at non-Faraday area (Fig. S6), where the Cdl of ZIF-67/PAN-800 $(9.14~\mathrm{mF/cm}^{2})$ ) is higher than that of ZIF-67-800 $(2.74~\mathrm{mF/cm}^{2})$ ) and PAN-800 $(6.16~\mathrm{mF/cm}^{2})$ ) (Figure 10 (a)), indicating the largest electrochemical active surface area (ECSA) for ZIF-67/PAN-800. The larger ECSA would provide the more electroactive sites, and facilitate the electrocatalytic activity34. The electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) were collected in Figure 10 (b). The resistance of ZIF-67/PAN-800 is lower than that of ZIF-67-800 and PAN-800, suggesting that the 1D structure of ZIF-67/PAN-800 was favorable for the transport of electronic. The large electrochemical active surface area and low EIS ZIF-67/PAN-800 were beneficial to its OER and ORR.
The stability of PAN-800, ZIF-67-800 and ZIF-67/PAN-800 was evaluated by i-t chronoamperometric measurements in $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ -saturated 0.1 M KOH with rotating rate of 1600 rpm. As shown in Figure 11, the relative current density of ZIF-67/PAN-800 $(89.7\%$ ) is higher than that of ZIF-67-800 $(77.0\%)$ and PAN-800 $(70.3\ \%)$ after $10\mathrm{{h}}$ continuous operation, indicating its excellent durability. For ZIF-67/PAN-800, as the Co particles were anchored on carbon fiber, this structure can prevent these Co particles from agglomerating and falling off during circulation. The result further conformed that the 1D structure was conducive to the cyclic stability of ZIF-67/PAN-800. The stability of catalyst is an important performance that influences its practical application35, thus the ZIF-67/PAN-800 serve as promising
electrocatalyst.
## 4. Conclusion
ZIF-67/PAN-800 has been successfully prepared via ZIF-67 in-situ grown on electrospun PAN fibers and followed by carbonization. ZIF-67/PAN-800 exhibits immobilized small cobalt nanoparticles, high porosity, and high nitrogen content, which is beneficial to catalyze ORR and OER. ZIF-67/PAN-800 exhibits excellent ORR catalytic performance which better than ZIF-67-800 and PAN-800 and comparable with the benchmark 20 wt% Pt/C, with a slightly negative half-wave potential of 0.81 V. The OER catalytic activity of ZIF-67/PAN-800 has a high current density of $45.1\ \mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ at 1.76 V. Such outstanding bifunctional catalytic activity can be due to the unique porous one-dimensional network structure and strong synergistic effect between ZIF-67 and PAN. The stability of ZIF-67/PAN-800 was better than that of ZIF-67, indicating that the structure of anchoring the metal particles by carbon fiber is beneficial for the stability. Therefore, these above-mentioned features enable ZIF-67/PAN-800 to serve as an effective and promising ORR/OER catalyst.
